1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,504
Every SAT grammar question comes
from a short list of thirteen rules.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:07,573
And most wrong answers
fall into four traps.

3
00:00:07,593 --> 00:00:08,645
A comma splice.

4
00:00:08,665 --> 00:00:12,235
A colon after a part that
isn't a complete sentence.

5
00:00:12,915 --> 00:00:15,236
A verb that matches the wrong noun.

6
00:00:15,486 --> 00:00:17,715
And the fourth one you can't even hear,

7
00:00:17,735 --> 00:00:21,272
because the right answer and the
wrong one sound exactly the same.

8
00:00:21,332 --> 00:00:25,705
The first three are quick to fix, and
each rule comes with a question to try.

9
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:27,475
The fourth is the apostrophe.

10
00:00:27,653 --> 00:00:29,760
That's rule thirteen, the last question.

11
00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:32,568
Stay for it, because reading
it out loud won't save you.

12
00:00:32,588 --> 00:00:34,623
That's the biggest mistake
on these questions:

13
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:36,516
picking the answer that sounds right.

14
00:00:37,016 --> 00:00:39,873
On the SAT, sounding right isn't the test.

15
00:00:40,123 --> 00:00:41,282
The rules are.

16
00:00:41,302 --> 00:00:43,623
Eight practice questions along the way.

17
00:00:43,658 --> 00:00:46,123
Keep score, and see if
you can get all eight.

18
00:00:46,148 --> 00:00:48,040
The rules split into two groups.

19
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:52,433
Part one is punctuation, which the
College Board calls Boundaries.

20
00:00:52,755 --> 00:00:55,627
Part two is grammar,
like agreement, verbs,

21
00:00:55,647 --> 00:00:58,735
and modifiers, which it
calls Form, Structure,

22
00:00:58,755 --> 00:00:59,678
and Sense.

23
00:00:59,698 --> 00:01:01,019
Part one.

24
00:01:01,269 --> 00:01:02,340
Boundaries.

25
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:07,518
These questions are all about where
one idea ends and the next one begins.

26
00:01:07,932 --> 00:01:08,932
Rule one.

27
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:14,697
Know what a complete sentence is, because
almost every punctuation rule depends

28
00:01:14,717 --> 00:01:15,410
on it.

29
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:18,231
A complete sentence, also
called an independent clause,

30
00:01:18,251 --> 00:01:22,680
has a subject, a verb,
and a complete thought.

31
00:01:23,358 --> 00:01:24,822
It can stand on its own.

32
00:01:24,848 --> 00:01:26,312
The bridge was closed.

33
00:01:27,241 --> 00:01:27,899
That's complete.

34
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:29,491
The subject is bridge.

35
00:01:29,669 --> 00:01:31,384
The verb is was closed.

36
00:01:31,427 --> 00:01:33,141
Now add one word.

37
00:01:33,426 --> 00:01:35,391
Because the bridge was closed.

38
00:01:35,749 --> 00:01:37,713
Suddenly it isn't complete.

39
00:01:38,249 --> 00:01:39,749
You're waiting for the rest.

40
00:01:39,856 --> 00:01:41,569
That's a dependent clause.

41
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:45,915
Words like because, although,
when, and while do that.

42
00:01:46,415 --> 00:01:49,236
So here's the test you'll use all video:

43
00:01:49,594 --> 00:01:52,308
could this part stand alone as a sentence?

44
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:54,665
If it can, it's independent.

45
00:01:54,741 --> 00:01:55,813
Rule two.

46
00:01:56,241 --> 00:01:59,007
You can't join two complete
sentences with just a comma.

47
00:01:59,027 --> 00:02:02,363
That's called a comma splice, and it's
one of the most common traps on these

48
00:02:02,383 --> 00:02:03,250
questions.

49
00:02:03,270 --> 00:02:06,554
There are three main ways to
join two complete sentences.

50
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:08,984
Use a period.

51
00:02:09,555 --> 00:02:10,841
Use a semicolon.

52
00:02:12,269 --> 00:02:15,091
Or use a comma plus a connecting word:

53
00:02:15,948 --> 00:02:20,035
for, and, nor, but, or, yet, and so.

54
00:02:20,055 --> 00:02:22,377
A lot of people remember those as FANBOYS.

55
00:02:22,409 --> 00:02:23,587
Let's try one.

56
00:02:24,623 --> 00:02:26,409
The octopus has three hearts.

57
00:02:27,052 --> 00:02:31,474
Two of them pump blood through the blank
pumps blood to the rest of the body.

58
00:02:31,494 --> 00:02:32,958
Pause here and pick your answer.

59
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:39,017
First, find the two complete sentences.

60
00:02:39,304 --> 00:02:41,697
Two of them pump blood through the gills.

61
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:42,840
Complete.

62
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:45,784
The third pumps blood
to the rest of the body.

63
00:02:45,804 --> 00:02:47,018
Also complete.

64
00:02:47,072 --> 00:02:50,785
A puts only a comma
between them: comma splice.

65
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,429
C has no punctuation: run-on.

66
00:02:53,965 --> 00:02:58,249
D puts the comma after and,
which is the wrong spot.

67
00:02:58,282 --> 00:03:01,333
Choice B puts a semicolon
between two complete sentences.

68
00:03:01,353 --> 00:03:02,389
That's the answer.

69
00:03:02,417 --> 00:03:03,417
Rule three.

70
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:05,969
A semicolon works exactly like a period.

71
00:03:05,989 --> 00:03:08,989
Whatever is on both sides has
to be a complete sentence.

72
00:03:09,032 --> 00:03:12,853
The experiment failed, semicolon,
the team still published its results.

73
00:03:13,211 --> 00:03:16,852
Both sides can stand alone,
so the semicolon works.

74
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:18,095
Now this one.

75
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:21,183
Although the experiment failed, semicolon,

76
00:03:21,203 --> 00:03:22,810
the team learned a lot.

77
00:03:23,488 --> 00:03:27,880
The first part starts with
although, so it can't stand alone.

78
00:03:28,130 --> 00:03:29,703
The semicolon is wrong.

79
00:03:32,417 --> 00:03:33,881
This sentence needs a comma.

80
00:03:33,948 --> 00:03:36,678
Quick check: swap the
semicolon for a period.

81
00:03:36,698 --> 00:03:39,162
If you get two real sentences, it works.

82
00:03:39,305 --> 00:03:43,035
The only other use is separating list
items that already have commas inside

83
00:03:43,055 --> 00:03:43,547
them.

84
00:03:43,567 --> 00:03:44,638
Rule four.

85
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:47,424
A colon needs a complete
sentence before it.

86
00:03:47,817 --> 00:03:51,419
After it comes a list, an
example, or an explanation.

87
00:03:51,439 --> 00:03:52,582
Here's the trap.

88
00:03:53,296 --> 00:03:56,491
The decree was written
in, colon, hieroglyphic,

89
00:03:56,511 --> 00:03:58,118
Demotic, and Greek.

90
00:03:58,368 --> 00:03:58,918
Written in what?

91
00:03:58,938 --> 00:04:02,204
The part before the colon isn't complete,

92
00:04:02,224 --> 00:04:03,761
so the colon is wrong.

93
00:04:04,796 --> 00:04:07,812
No colons right after
a verb, a preposition,

94
00:04:07,832 --> 00:04:10,761
or words like including and such as.

95
00:04:10,810 --> 00:04:11,845
One more thing.

96
00:04:12,274 --> 00:04:14,952
A single dash can do
the same job as a colon.

97
00:04:15,595 --> 00:04:19,380
It can introduce a list or an
explanation after a complete sentence.

98
00:04:19,431 --> 00:04:20,574
Try this one.

99
00:04:22,323 --> 00:04:27,509
The Rosetta Stone carries the same decree
written in three blank hieroglyphic,

100
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:29,421
Demotic, and ancient Greek.

101
00:04:29,514 --> 00:04:30,943
Pause and try it.

102
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:37,413
Before the blank: The Rosetta Stone
carries the same decree written in three

103
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:38,433
scripts.

104
00:04:38,755 --> 00:04:39,969
Complete sentence.

105
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:42,255
After it: a list.

106
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:46,643
A complete sentence followed by a
list is exactly what a colon is for.

107
00:04:46,663 --> 00:04:51,322
B uses a semicolon, but the list
after it isn't a complete sentence.

108
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:52,858
C has no punctuation at all.

109
00:04:52,878 --> 00:04:55,485
And D creates a comma splice.

110
00:04:55,628 --> 00:04:57,663
The answer is A.

111
00:04:57,706 --> 00:04:58,885
Rule five.

112
00:04:59,206 --> 00:05:02,758
Words like however, therefore, moreover,

113
00:05:02,778 --> 00:05:05,278
and for example are transitions.

114
00:05:05,635 --> 00:05:09,385
They are not connecting
words like and or but,

115
00:05:09,564 --> 00:05:13,064
so they can't join two complete
sentences with just commas.

116
00:05:13,118 --> 00:05:14,297
Compare these.

117
00:05:15,047 --> 00:05:17,440
The test was hard, but she finished early.

118
00:05:18,154 --> 00:05:21,654
That's correct, because
but is one of the FANBOYS.

119
00:05:22,189 --> 00:05:25,205
The test was hard, however,
she finished early.

120
00:05:25,225 --> 00:05:27,046
That's a comma splice.

121
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:29,404
However can't connect two sentences.

122
00:05:29,449 --> 00:05:32,428
The fix is a semicolon or
a period before however,

123
00:05:32,448 --> 00:05:34,020
and a comma after it.

124
00:05:34,663 --> 00:05:37,393
The test was hard, semicolon, however,

125
00:05:37,413 --> 00:05:39,306
comma, she finished early.

126
00:05:39,357 --> 00:05:40,357
One note.

127
00:05:40,714 --> 00:05:44,929
Commas around however are fine when it
sits in the middle of a single sentence,

128
00:05:45,357 --> 00:05:48,500
like, the results, however, were clear.

129
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,643
The problem is only when however
sits between two complete sentences.

130
00:05:52,724 --> 00:05:53,724
Your turn.

131
00:05:54,046 --> 00:05:59,320
Many people assume that bats are
blank every bat species can see,

132
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:03,162
and many species also use
echolocation to navigate in the dark.

133
00:06:03,234 --> 00:06:04,663
Pause and try it.

134
00:06:07,903 --> 00:06:11,545
Both sides are complete sentences,
and however sits between them.

135
00:06:11,832 --> 00:06:13,990
So we need a semicolon before however,

136
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:15,546
and a comma after it.

137
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:18,082
A and D are comma splices.

138
00:06:18,617 --> 00:06:20,760
C has nothing before however.

139
00:06:21,010 --> 00:06:22,296
The answer is B.

140
00:06:22,335 --> 00:06:23,371
Rule six.

141
00:06:24,192 --> 00:06:27,708
Extra information, the kind you could
delete without breaking the sentence,

142
00:06:27,728 --> 00:06:28,942
gets wrapped in a matching pair.

143
00:06:29,121 --> 00:06:30,101
Two commas.

144
00:06:30,121 --> 00:06:30,886
Two dashes.

145
00:06:30,906 --> 00:06:31,906
Or two parentheses.

146
00:06:31,996 --> 00:06:36,011
This is what the SAT loves to
test: the pair has to match.

147
00:06:36,031 --> 00:06:38,797
If it opens with a comma,
it closes with a comma.

148
00:06:38,817 --> 00:06:41,496
If it opens with a dash,
it closes with a dash.

149
00:06:41,582 --> 00:06:42,689
Here's how to check.

150
00:06:44,188 --> 00:06:45,618
Delete the extra part.

151
00:06:46,832 --> 00:06:49,582
If what's left is still a
complete, correct sentence,

152
00:06:50,117 --> 00:06:51,795
you've found the right boundaries.

153
00:06:51,841 --> 00:06:52,841
Try it.

154
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:59,000
Mary Anning, a largely self-taught fossil
collector from Lyme Regis blank made

155
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:03,657
some of the most important fossil
discoveries of the early nineteenth century.

156
00:07:03,677 --> 00:07:05,106
Pause and try it.

157
00:07:08,346 --> 00:07:11,147
The extra information opens
with a comma after Mary Anning,

158
00:07:11,167 --> 00:07:12,933
so it has to close with a comma.

159
00:07:12,953 --> 00:07:16,540
Delete the middle: Mary Anning made
some of the most important fossil

160
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:19,325
discoveries of the early
nineteenth century.

161
00:07:19,345 --> 00:07:20,056
Clean.

162
00:07:20,076 --> 00:07:21,628
A mixes a comma with a dash.

163
00:07:21,648 --> 00:07:25,683
C uses a semicolon, which leaves
pieces that can't stand alone.

164
00:07:25,862 --> 00:07:27,735
D never closes the pair.

165
00:07:27,755 --> 00:07:29,362
The answer is B.

166
00:07:29,439 --> 00:07:32,367
Rule seven is about where
punctuation does not go.

167
00:07:33,296 --> 00:07:36,546
Plenty of wrong answers add
punctuation where none belongs.

168
00:07:36,593 --> 00:07:39,735
Never put a single comma
between a subject and its verb.

169
00:07:40,307 --> 00:07:42,466
The students who finished early, comma,

170
00:07:42,486 --> 00:07:43,700
left the room.

171
00:07:44,807 --> 00:07:45,751
Wrong.

172
00:07:45,771 --> 00:07:48,251
The subject is the students
who finished early.

173
00:07:48,271 --> 00:07:49,394
The verb is left.

174
00:07:49,414 --> 00:07:50,914
No comma between them.

175
00:07:50,969 --> 00:07:53,218
Same with a verb and what follows it.

176
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:58,612
The study found, comma,
that sleep improves memory.

177
00:07:59,862 --> 00:08:00,862
Found what?

178
00:08:01,825 --> 00:08:03,718
That sleep improves memory.

179
00:08:04,112 --> 00:08:05,052
No comma.

180
00:08:05,072 --> 00:08:08,642
And no commas around a name when the
name tells you which person you mean.

181
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:13,444
In 1993, novelist Toni
Morrison won the Nobel Prize in

182
00:08:13,464 --> 00:08:14,464
Literature.

183
00:08:14,822 --> 00:08:18,750
No commas around Toni Morrison, because
the name tells you which novelist.

184
00:08:18,849 --> 00:08:19,849
Part two.

185
00:08:19,957 --> 00:08:22,242
Form, Structure, and Sense.

186
00:08:23,099 --> 00:08:25,758
These questions test
whether words fit together:

187
00:08:25,778 --> 00:08:28,472
subjects and verbs, pronouns and nouns,

188
00:08:28,492 --> 00:08:32,064
verbs and time, and modifiers
and what they describe.

189
00:08:32,097 --> 00:08:33,097
Rule eight.

190
00:08:34,311 --> 00:08:36,597
A verb has to match its subject.

191
00:08:36,882 --> 00:08:39,524
A singular subject gets a singular verb.

192
00:08:39,667 --> 00:08:41,846
A plural subject gets a plural verb.

193
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:46,446
The SAT makes this hard by stuffing
words between the subject and the verb.

194
00:08:46,946 --> 00:08:51,246
So cross out everything in between,
especially phrases starting with words like

195
00:08:51,266 --> 00:08:53,553
of, in, and with.

196
00:08:53,621 --> 00:08:54,656
Try this one.

197
00:08:56,406 --> 00:08:58,101
The collection of letters, photographs,

198
00:08:58,121 --> 00:09:02,779
and diaries donated to the museum by
the artist's family blank a rare look at

199
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:03,871
her early career.

200
00:09:03,959 --> 00:09:05,387
Pause and try it.

201
00:09:08,628 --> 00:09:11,592
Cross out of letters,
photographs, and diaries.

202
00:09:11,806 --> 00:09:15,378
Cross out donated to the
museum by the artist's family.

203
00:09:15,877 --> 00:09:16,984
What's left?

204
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:21,628
The collection, blank, a rare look.

205
00:09:22,199 --> 00:09:23,878
The subject is collection.

206
00:09:23,913 --> 00:09:25,199
One collection.

207
00:09:25,485 --> 00:09:26,485
Singular.

208
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:30,816
Offer, have offered, and
are offering are all plural.

209
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:34,510
They're tempting because
plural nouns like letters,

210
00:09:34,530 --> 00:09:38,280
photographs, and diaries sit
between the subject and the verb.

211
00:09:38,816 --> 00:09:42,209
The answer is B, offers.

212
00:09:42,242 --> 00:09:43,242
Rule nine.

213
00:09:43,742 --> 00:09:46,436
A pronoun has to match
the noun it replaces.

214
00:09:46,456 --> 00:09:48,634
One thing gets it or its.

215
00:09:49,455 --> 00:09:51,742
More than one gets they or their.

216
00:09:51,775 --> 00:09:54,290
The SAT's favorite trap is a group noun,

217
00:09:54,310 --> 00:09:56,918
like a company, a team, or a committee.

218
00:09:57,454 --> 00:10:00,382
On the SAT, those are singular.

219
00:10:01,025 --> 00:10:04,862
After years of research, the company
finally released their first electric

220
00:10:04,882 --> 00:10:05,882
truck.

221
00:10:06,168 --> 00:10:07,168
Wrong.

222
00:10:07,775 --> 00:10:10,704
One company, so it's its
first electric truck.

223
00:10:10,775 --> 00:10:13,612
Whenever pronouns change
across the answer choices,

224
00:10:13,632 --> 00:10:17,346
find the noun the pronoun
points to, and ask one question:

225
00:10:17,953 --> 00:10:21,417
one, or more than one?

226
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:22,460
Rule ten.

227
00:10:23,353 --> 00:10:26,388
Verb tense has to match the
timeline of the passage.

228
00:10:26,853 --> 00:10:29,583
The choices are usually the
same verb in different tenses,

229
00:10:29,603 --> 00:10:32,102
so your job is to find the time clue.

230
00:10:32,161 --> 00:10:33,268
Try this one.

231
00:10:34,232 --> 00:10:39,126
In 1903, Marie Curie became the
first woman to win a Nobel Prize.

232
00:10:39,803 --> 00:10:43,677
Eight years later, she
blank a second Nobel Prize,

233
00:10:43,697 --> 00:10:45,554
this time in chemistry.

234
00:10:45,593 --> 00:10:47,021
Pause and try it.

235
00:10:50,262 --> 00:10:52,170
The time clues are in 1903,

236
00:10:52,190 --> 00:10:53,598
and eight years later.

237
00:10:53,618 --> 00:10:56,655
That's a specific, finished
moment in the past,

238
00:10:56,869 --> 00:10:58,583
so we need the simple past.

239
00:10:58,762 --> 00:11:01,762
The answer is C, won.

240
00:11:01,866 --> 00:11:03,667
The trap is D, has won.

241
00:11:03,687 --> 00:11:06,739
That tense links the past to the present,

242
00:11:06,759 --> 00:11:10,330
so it can't go with a specific
past time like eight years later.

243
00:11:10,902 --> 00:11:13,973
Wins and will win are
the wrong time entirely.

244
00:11:14,019 --> 00:11:15,055
Rule eleven.

245
00:11:16,125 --> 00:11:18,912
Every sentence needs a real main verb.

246
00:11:19,412 --> 00:11:23,733
Words like stretching or being can't
be the main verb by themselves.

247
00:11:24,162 --> 00:11:26,733
Neither can to plus a
verb, like to stretch.

248
00:11:26,802 --> 00:11:28,282
Look at this.

249
00:11:28,302 --> 00:11:31,461
The Great Barrier Reef, stretching
more than 2,000 kilometers along the

250
00:11:31,481 --> 00:11:35,552
coast of Australia, being the largest
coral reef system in the world.

251
00:11:36,159 --> 00:11:37,159
Read it again.

252
00:11:37,767 --> 00:11:39,623
There's no main verb.

253
00:11:39,767 --> 00:11:42,445
Stretching and being can't do the job.

254
00:11:42,484 --> 00:11:45,626
Change being to is, and it works.

255
00:11:46,198 --> 00:11:50,071
The Great Barrier Reef is the largest
coral reef system in the world.

256
00:11:50,091 --> 00:11:52,427
If every choice looks fine on its own,

257
00:11:52,447 --> 00:11:55,019
check whether the sentence
even has a main verb.

258
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:56,319
Rule twelve.

259
00:11:57,284 --> 00:12:00,049
When a sentence starts with a
describing phrase and a comma,

260
00:12:00,069 --> 00:12:03,248
the very next words have to
name the thing being described.

261
00:12:03,292 --> 00:12:05,828
Running down the street,
my backpack fell open.

262
00:12:06,507 --> 00:12:08,022
That says the backpack was running.

263
00:12:08,042 --> 00:12:12,114
The fix: Running down the street,
I felt my backpack fall open.

264
00:12:13,256 --> 00:12:15,399
Now the runner comes
right after the comma.

265
00:12:15,419 --> 00:12:17,455
Here's an SAT-style version.

266
00:12:18,169 --> 00:12:21,497
Originally developed for the
United States military blank.

267
00:12:21,517 --> 00:12:23,124
Read all four choices.

268
00:12:23,147 --> 00:12:24,575
Pause and try it.

269
00:12:28,816 --> 00:12:32,223
Ask yourself: what was originally
developed for the military?

270
00:12:32,243 --> 00:12:34,617
The Global Positioning System.

271
00:12:34,637 --> 00:12:38,637
So the Global Positioning System
has to come right after the comma.

272
00:12:38,922 --> 00:12:40,459
Only choice A does that.

273
00:12:40,548 --> 00:12:44,083
B and C put people and
the world after the comma,

274
00:12:44,476 --> 00:12:47,884
which would mean they were
developed for the military.

275
00:12:47,904 --> 00:12:51,563
D starts with there are, which
doesn't name anything at all.

276
00:12:51,583 --> 00:12:53,539
The answer is A.

277
00:12:53,559 --> 00:12:54,881
Rule thirteen.

278
00:12:55,308 --> 00:13:00,881
Apostrophes show ownership,
or a shortened word like it's.

279
00:13:01,559 --> 00:13:03,308
They never make a word plural.

280
00:13:03,331 --> 00:13:06,902
Bees, with no apostrophe,
just means more than one bee.

281
00:13:06,974 --> 00:13:09,795
Bee apostrophe s means
belonging to one bee.

282
00:13:09,867 --> 00:13:12,881
Bees apostrophe means
belonging to more than one bee.

283
00:13:12,901 --> 00:13:16,237
And the one to memorize:
it's, with an apostrophe,

284
00:13:16,257 --> 00:13:17,579
means it is.

285
00:13:18,115 --> 00:13:20,793
Its, with no apostrophe,
means belonging to it.

286
00:13:20,878 --> 00:13:22,092
Last question.

287
00:13:22,378 --> 00:13:27,020
When a honeybee finds food, she returns
to the hive and performs a waggle dance.

288
00:13:27,378 --> 00:13:32,378
The direction of the blank tells other
bees which way to fly to reach the food.

289
00:13:32,422 --> 00:13:33,851
Pause and try it.

290
00:13:37,091 --> 00:13:38,913
The passage is about one bee.

291
00:13:39,020 --> 00:13:41,321
A honeybee finds food, and
she performs the dance.

292
00:13:41,341 --> 00:13:44,091
So one bee owns the waggle run.

293
00:13:44,269 --> 00:13:45,841
That's bee apostrophe s.

294
00:13:45,877 --> 00:13:47,376
The answer is A.

295
00:13:47,424 --> 00:13:50,424
B has no apostrophe, so
nothing owns the waggle run.

296
00:13:50,638 --> 00:13:54,189
C means more than one bee, but
the passage is about just one.

297
00:13:54,209 --> 00:13:56,154
And D adds an apostrophe to waggle run,

298
00:13:56,174 --> 00:13:57,531
which doesn't own anything.

299
00:13:57,591 --> 00:13:59,413
That's all thirteen rules.

300
00:13:59,662 --> 00:14:01,821
Now, here's how to use them on test day,

301
00:14:01,841 --> 00:14:03,052
in three steps.

302
00:14:03,072 --> 00:14:04,179
Step one.

303
00:14:04,250 --> 00:14:06,500
Look at what changes between the choices.

304
00:14:07,036 --> 00:14:08,215
Punctuation?

305
00:14:08,965 --> 00:14:10,230
It's a Boundaries question.

306
00:14:10,250 --> 00:14:11,250
A verb?

307
00:14:11,715 --> 00:14:13,301
Check agreement and tense.

308
00:14:13,321 --> 00:14:14,429
A pronoun?

309
00:14:14,786 --> 00:14:16,465
Find what it refers to.

310
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:17,684
Step two.

311
00:14:17,755 --> 00:14:20,521
Find the structure:
the complete sentences,

312
00:14:20,541 --> 00:14:24,398
the real subject, the time
clue, or who's doing the action.

313
00:14:24,447 --> 00:14:25,554
Step three.

314
00:14:25,661 --> 00:14:28,483
Eliminate using the rules, not your ear.

315
00:14:29,125 --> 00:14:31,427
If you can't name the
rule an answer breaks,

316
00:14:31,447 --> 00:14:32,768
don't cross it out yet.

317
00:14:32,811 --> 00:14:34,490
And one bonus tip.

318
00:14:34,740 --> 00:14:37,863
If two choices do the exact same job,

319
00:14:37,883 --> 00:14:40,219
like a period and a
semicolon in the same spot,

320
00:14:40,239 --> 00:14:43,347
they can't both be the one right answer.

321
00:14:43,704 --> 00:14:46,596
So neither one is.

322
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:49,019
Look at what makes the
other choices different.

323
00:14:49,039 --> 00:14:53,146
That's all thirteen, on one screen.

324
00:14:53,754 --> 00:14:55,396
Pause here if you want a screenshot.

325
00:14:55,427 --> 00:14:59,622
Pretty much every grammar question
you'll see tests one of these.

326
00:14:59,642 --> 00:15:03,193
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

327
00:15:03,213 --> 00:15:04,891
Standard English Conventions.

328
00:15:04,925 --> 00:15:08,083
All the practice questions in this
video were written for this video,

329
00:15:08,103 --> 00:15:10,246
in the style of the real test.

330
00:15:10,389 --> 00:15:12,175
If this helped, subscribe.

331
